  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nIf a `focus` event handler moves the focus to another element while `focus()` is running, the spec scrolls the original target into view at the end, so the element that actually ends up focused is scrolled out of view:\n\n```html\n<div id=\"a\" tabindex=\"-1\" style=\"margin-top: 200vh\"></div>\n<div id=\"b\" tabindex=\"-1\" style=\"margin-top: 200vh\"></div>\n<script>\n  a.addEventListener(\"focus\", () => b.focus());\n  a.focus();\n</script>\n```\n\n1. `a.focus()` runs the [focusing steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#focusing-steps), which fire `focus` at `a`.\n2. The handler calls `b.focus()`, which focuses `b` and scrolls it into view.\n3. Back in `a.focus()`, [step 4](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#dom-focusoptions-preventscroll) scrolls `a` into view.\n\nHowever, browsers don't agree with this, and `b` is focused in all of them (except Servo).\nChromium and Firefox scroll the target into view before firing `focus`, so the handler's `b.focus()` runs afterwards.\nWebKit and Ladybird scroll after the focus events, but skip it when the focus didn't end up on the target.\n\nI guess spec should be modified to follow browsers' behavior.",
